---
source: crates/enth-cli/tests/render_bpm.rs
expression: flags
---
 enth render [OPTIONS] <PATCH>

Arguments:
  <PATCH>
          The patch file

Options:
      --format <FORMAT>
          Output format (default: json when stdout is not a terminal, table when it is)

          Possible values:
          - json:  One JSON object (the default when stdout is not a terminal)
          - table: Tables for people (the default on a terminal)

      --perf <PERF>
          The performance: a file, inline JSON (`enth schema perf`), `-` for stdin, or `note=A3,vel=100,hold=1.5s,dur=2.5s`

      --note <NOTE>
          The note played (`C3`, `A#4`, or a MIDI number). Default C3

      --vel <VEL>
          MIDI velocity 1-127. Default 100

      --hold <TIME>
          How long the note is held (`1.5s`, `800ms`, or seconds). Default 1.5 s

      --dur <TIME>
          Length of the clip (`3s`, or seconds); it must cover the release. Default 3 s, or hold + 1.5 s

      --out <WAV>
          The WAV to write (default: the patch path with .wav)

      --bits <BITS>
          Sample format: f32 (default), 16 or 24

          Possible values:
          - f32: 32-bit IEEE float (the default): the engine's samples exactly, peaks above 0 dBFS kept
          - 16:  16-bit integer PCM, clipped at full scale
          - 24:  24-bit integer PCM, clipped at full scale
          
          [default: f32]

      --seed <SEED>
          The render seed (default: the fixed default seed)

      --bpm <BPM>
          The tempo in beats per minute, 20 to 999: what a tempo-synced module (an lfo with sync on) follows; a beat is a quarter note. A patch with no synced module renders the same at every tempo
          
          [default: 140]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')
